OpenCobolIDE 4.7.6 modern Debian maintenance cleanup
====================================================

Purpose
-------

This cleanup makes the modern-python branch a focused Debian-family Linux
repository. The repository remains based on the original OpenCobolIDE 4.7.6
code and is not a new upstream release.

Backup
------

The state before this cleanup is preserved in the branch:

    codex/legacy-cross-platform-backup

That branch retains the former Windows and macOS build scripts, platform
assets, Windows GnuCOBOL submodule reference, RPM configuration, and legacy CI
configuration.

Additions and updates
---------------------

* Made README.md the packaging long description and declared Markdown format.
* Included Markdown files in source distributions.
* Included this change record and excluded generated Python bytecode from
  source distributions.
* Updated the functional test to open README.md.
* Updated project, help, issue-reporting, and Debian package URLs to the
  maintained ikonomn/OpenCobolIDE repository.
* Updated Debian build prerequisites and maintainer metadata.
* Replaced obsolete installation and contribution documentation with guidance
  for Debian-family Linux, Python 3.8+, PyQt5 5.15+, and GnuCOBOL.
* Marked remaining cross-platform feature documentation and upstream release
  notes as historical rather than supported build targets.
* Removed deprecated setuptools test-command integration.
* Added this change record.

Removals
--------

* Removed the obsolete Travis CI configuration for Ubuntu Trusty, Python 3.4,
  PyQt4, and OpenCOBOL.
* Removed Windows and macOS release scripts and launcher files.
* Removed the Windows GnuCOBOL submodule reference.
* Removed macOS and Windows packaging assets that are not installed by the
  Debian package.
* Removed obsolete RPM packaging configuration.
* Removed bundled dependency test suites, test certificates, and test keys
  that are not needed at runtime.
* Removed the Python enum backport because Python 3.8+ provides enum.

Files intentionally retained
----------------------------

The bundled PyQode, Pygments, QCrash, QDarkStyle, Keyring, and future modules
remain because application code still imports them. Further dependency
replacement should be handled separately and tested as an application change.
The small platform-specific Keyring backend modules are also retained because
the legacy Keyring loader discovers them dynamically even when they are not
usable on the current operating system.
